🔧 Demo Use Case: E-commerce (Choreography + Kafka)
Services: Order, Payment, Inventory

Happy path events:
1) OrderCreated
2) PaymentCompleted
3) InventoryReserved / OrderCompleted

Failure/compensation events:
- PaymentFailed
- InventoryFailed → then refund payment + cancel order

Topics (example):
- `payment-topic`
- `inventory-topic`
- `order-topic`


🧱 1. Order Service (starts saga)
```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public OrderService(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void createOrder(Order order) throws Exception {
        // 1) local TX: save order as PENDING
        // 2) publish event for next step
        String payload = new ObjectMapper().writeValueAsString(order);
        kafkaTemplate.send("payment-topic", payload);
    }
}
```

🧱 2. Payment Service
```java
@Component
public class PaymentConsumer {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public PaymentConsumer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "payment-topic", groupId = "payment-service")
    public void handlePayment(String message) throws Exception {
        Order order = new ObjectMapper().readValue(message, Order.class);

        boolean paymentSuccess = charge(order); // local TX

        if (paymentSuccess) {
            kafkaTemplate.send("inventory-topic", message);
        } else {
            kafkaTemplate.send("order-topic", "PAYMENT_FAILED:" + order.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

🧱 3. Inventory Service
```java
@Component
public class InventoryConsumer {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public InventoryConsumer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "inventory-topic", groupId = "inventory-service")
    public void handleInventory(String message) throws Exception {
        Order order = new ObjectMapper().readValue(message, Order.class);

        boolean inStock = reserveStock(order); // local TX

        if (inStock) {
            kafkaTemplate.send("order-topic", "ORDER_COMPLETED:" + order.getId());
        } else {
            // payment already succeeded → compensation needed
            kafkaTemplate.send("order-topic", "INVENTORY_FAILED:" + order.getId());
            // also emit event so PaymentService can refund
            kafkaTemplate.send("payment-topic", "REFUND:" + order.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

🔄 Order Service compensation / completion listener
```java
@KafkaListener(topics = "order-topic", groupId = "order-service")
public void handleSagaResponse(String event) {
    if (event.startsWith("PAYMENT_FAILED") || event.startsWith("INVENTORY_FAILED")) {
        // local TX: mark order CANCELED
        System.out.println("Compensating: cancel order for " + event);
    } else if (event.startsWith("ORDER_COMPLETED")) {
        // local TX: mark order CONFIRMED
        System.out.println("Order completed: " + event);
    }
}
```

Note: this is a teaching demo (string events). Real systems use typed events + clear compensation topics/commands.


🔐 Key interview points
1) Saga ≠ ACID across services; it is eventual consistency
2) Compensation = business undo (refund/cancel/release), not DB rollback across services
3) Idempotency is mandatory (duplicate events/retries)
4) Prefer orchestration when flow is long/complex; choreography for simple short flows
5) Reliable publishing often needs Outbox pattern (DB commit + event safely)
